<Base title="When the time comes" description="Quality of life, the decision, the day itself, and the practical steps afterwards. Written for owners of an old or seriously ill animal.">
  <article class="wrap narrow hero prose">
    <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/animals">Animal information</a> · End of life</p>
    <h1>When the time comes</h1>
    <p class="lead">Nobody wants to read this page. If you are here, you are already doing the hard part: thinking about it before you have to. This is what veterinarians wish owners knew earlier.</p>

    <h2>Quality of life, day by day</h2>
    <p>The question is rarely "is it time?" on one dramatic day. It is a slow drift that is hard to see from inside. Veterinarians use structured questions so the drift becomes visible. The best-known set is Dr Alice Villalobos's seven categories, often called the HHHHHMM scale: <strong>hurt</strong>, <strong>hunger</strong>, <strong>hydration</strong>, <strong>hygiene</strong>, <strong>happiness</strong>, <strong>mobility</strong>, and <strong>more good days than bad</strong>. Your vet can score it with you; the categories alone are useful at home.</p>
    <ul class="paw-list">
      <li>Keep a simple diary: each evening, was today a good day or a bad day, and why. The <a href="/passport">health booklet</a> has a symptom diary for exactly this. Three bad days in a row, or a week where bad days outnumber good ones, is a reason to call.</li>
      <li>Write down three things your animal loves. When two of the three are gone and not coming back, say so to your vet. That sentence is often the start of the conversation.</li>
      <li>Pain hides. Cats stop jumping and grooming; dogs stop greeting you at the door and lick one spot. Neither cries. Your vet can tell you what pain looks like in your animal.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The conversation with your vet</h2>
    <p>Ask directly: "What would you do if this were your animal?" Most vets answer honestly. Ask what the next weeks look like with treatment and without, what a crisis would look like, and what you should do at 2 a.m. if it happens. Ask whether a home visit is possible for the last appointment; many practices offer it, and some mobile vets do only that.</p>
    <p>Deciding a little early is a kindness. Deciding late is the regret vets hear most often.</p>

    <h2>The day itself</h2>
    <ul class="paw-list">
      <li>You can stay. You can also choose not to; both are fine, and your vet has seen both many times.</li>
      <li>Ask how it works before it starts. Usually a sedative first, so your animal is asleep, then the final injection. It is quick and calm. Eyes may stay open; there may be a breath or a twitch afterwards. That is the body, not the animal.</li>
      <li>Bring a blanket from home. Decide beforehand about a paw print or a lock of fur; the practice will offer, and it is easier to have decided.</li>
      <li>Children cope better when told the truth in simple words and given a choice about being there.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Afterwards: the practical list</h2>
    <ul class="paw-list">
      <li><strong>Body.</strong> Cremation (individual or communal) through the practice, or burial where local rules allow it. Rules differ by country and municipality; the practice knows the local ones.</li>
      <li><strong>Registry.</strong> Report the death to the microchip registry. The deadlines and routes per country are on the <a href="/registry">registries page</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Insurance and subscriptions.</strong> Cancel the policy, food deliveries, parasite-prevention subscriptions and any reminders.</li>
      <li><strong>The booklet.</strong> Keep it. A year later, people are glad they have the dates and the photo. Nothing here is deleted unless you delete it.</li>
      <li><strong>You.</strong> Grief for an animal is grief. If it is heavy, say so to someone; some countries have pet-loss support lines run by veterinary charities, and your vet will know them.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>If anything on this page raises a question about your own animal, the person to ask is your vet. They have been through this with other families and will go through it with you.</p>
